A <representation of a functor> $F$ is $(A,x)$ with $x\in F(A)$ such that $h\mapsto F(h)(x)$ is a <bijection> $\mathcal C(A,B)\to F(B)$ for every $B$. These <bijections> are natural by functoriality. The element $x$ is its <universal element>.

If $(B,y)$ is another representation, universality gives unique arrows $f:A\to B$ and $g:B\to A$ satisfying $F(f)x=y$ and $F(g)y=x$. Consequently $F(gf)x=x=F(1_A)x$, so $gf=1_A$ by injectivity of the representing <bijection>. Similarly $fg=1_B$. Therefore $f$ is the unique compatible <isomorphism>. This proves <uniqueness of functor representations>; uniqueness refers to an <isomorphism> carrying the specified <universal elements> to one another, rather than to every <isomorphism> between the underlying objects.

